# ─── ShipShow! / bbstat — container env ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Copy this file to `.env.docker` (gitignored) and fill in the BitBucket
# credentials. Loaded by the migrate, backend, and sync services via
# `env_file:` in compose.yaml; the frontend image bakes its config at build
# time and does NOT read this file.
#
# What's NOT here, and why:
#   * DATABASE_URL / REDIS_URL — supplied by compose. In dev these point at
#     the internal postgres/redis services (compose.override.yaml); in
#     production they come from the deploy environment as required vars.
#   * TLS_KEY_PATH / TLS_CERT_PATH — containers run with BACKEND_TLS=off
#     behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy (Traefik in dev, the deployer's
#     proxy in prod). The backend never sees a certificate.
#   * VITE_* — baked into the frontend bundle at image-build time; runtime
#     overrides have no effect on a built SPA.
#   * SEED_REPO_FILE_PATH — pinned by compose.yaml to /app/tracked-repos.yaml.
#     Bind-mount the host file at that path to load seeds.
#
# Anything below is also overridable per-service via compose's `environment:`
# block; values there beat values from this file, per Compose's standard
# precedence rules.

# ─── Image tags (production deploys) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Pin the exact image versions the stack runs. Defaults to `:latest` (handy
# for local dev), but production should always pin a real tag so rollbacks
# and reproducibility work. Tag format is whatever your build pipeline emits
# (e.g. `bbstat-backend:v1.2.0`, `:git-sha-abc123`, …). The compose.yaml
# never builds — production loads images via `docker load -i …` and these
# vars point compose at them by name.
# BACKEND_IMAGE=bbstat-backend:latest
# FRONTEND_IMAGE=bbstat-frontend:latest

# ─── Runtime ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=production

# Backend listen port inside the container. The compose network reaches
# backend at <service-name>:8080 (matched by the frontend nginx upstream and
# by the backend image's healthcheck). Override only if you re-wire both.
BACKEND_PORT=8080

# Plain HTTP listener — the reverse proxy is the public TLS edge. Don't flip
# to 'on' in containers; the cert paths aren't bind-mounted and startup will
# fail trying to read them.
BACKEND_TLS=off

# ─── BitBucket API (sync) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Workspace and base URL stay here (not secrets — just deployment config).
#
# BITBUCKET_AUTH_EMAIL and BITBUCKET_AUTH_TOKEN are intentionally NOT in this
# file. compose.yaml interpolates them from the host shell ENV so secrets
# never need to land on disk. Production pattern:
#
#     # in deploy.env (NOT committed, deleted after sourcing)
#     export BITBUCKET_AUTH_EMAIL=you@example.com
#     export BITBUCKET_AUTH_TOKEN=ATATT3xFf…
#     export DATABASE_URL=postgres://…
#     export REDIS_URL=redis://…
#     export MCP_TOKEN_SECRET=…          # required if you run the `mcp` service
#
#     set -a; source deploy.env; set +a; rm -f deploy.env
#     docker compose -f compose.yaml up -d
#
# For dev (`make up`), set the same vars in the project-root `.env` file —
# compose auto-loads it for interpolation, so they're picked up without any
# extra sourcing step. Leaving them unset gracefully disables sync and
# /api/admin routes; the dashboard still serves previously ingested data.
BITBUCKET_BASE_URL=https://api.bitbucket.org/2.0
BITBUCKET_WORKSPACE=

# Outbound BB API budget — the client self-paces against these, since BB
# Cloud doesn't return X-RateLimit-* headers on success. Defaults match the
# documented ~1000/hr cap with a 400ms floor to dodge the sub-second burst
# limiter (which 429s above ~2-3 calls/sec).
BITBUCKET_HOURLY_CALL_BUDGET=1000
BITBUCKET_MIN_CALL_INTERVAL_MS=400
BITBUCKET_MAX_CALL_INTERVAL_MS=3200

# ─── Cron schedules (override only if defaults are too tight) ──────────────
# SYNC_MEMBERS_CRON=*/10 * * * *
# SYNC_REPOS_CRON=*/5 * * * *
# SYNC_COMMITS_CRON=*/5 * * * *
# SYNC_PRS_ENABLED=false
# SYNC_PRS_CRON=0 * * * *
# Auto-track workspace repos (off by default). When true, the repos-cron sweep
# walks GET /repositories/<workspace> by updated_on and tracks untracked repos.
# Preview anytime with `POST /api/admin/sync/discover-repos` (dry-run default).
# REPO_DISCOVERY_ENABLED=false

# ─── First-run commit backfill caps (no effect on delta runs) ──────────────
# COMMITS_BACKFILL_MAX_PAGES=5
# COMMITS_BACKFILL_SINCE=               # ISO 8601 (e.g. 2026-01-01) or empty
# COMMIT_SYNC_CONCURRENCY=3
# COMMIT_SYNC_BUDGET_THRESHOLD=20

# ─── Tick orchestration knobs ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# See apps/backend/src/config.ts for the full list and per-knob rationale.
# Defaults are tuned for the TigerIT workspace (~30 tracked repos); raise
# or lower per your scale and the BB hourly budget.
# DIFF_STATS_BUDGET_THRESHOLD=10
# DIFF_STATS_BACKFILL_MAX_COMMITS_PER_TICK=100
# DIFF_STATS_BACKFILL_BUDGET_THRESHOLD=20
# IS_MERGE_BACKFILL_MAX_PAGES=5
# IS_MERGE_BACKFILL_BUDGET_THRESHOLD=50
# DEEPEN_BACKFILL_MAX_PAGES=5
# DEEPEN_BUDGET_THRESHOLD=100
# Grace window (hours) before a still-empty repo is auto-excluded from sync.
# Keeps a freshly-added repo in active sync until its first push lands; set
# to 0 to exclude empty repos immediately (old behavior).
# SYNC_EMPTY_GRACE_HOURS=24
# PR_BACKFILL_MAX_PAGES=5
# PR_SYNC_CONCURRENCY=3
# PR_SYNC_BUDGET_THRESHOLD=20
# SYNC_BACKOFF_SLOPE_MIN_PER_QUIET_DAY=4
# SYNC_BACKOFF_MIN_INTERVAL_MIN=5
# SYNC_BACKOFF_MAX_INTERVAL_MIN=1440
# Cap on never-synced ("cold") repos admitted per commit/PR tick. Warm
# (already-synced) active repos are serviced first and in full; cold repos
# trickle in behind them so a large discovery backlog backfills gradually
# instead of starving the active rotation. Raise to drain a backlog faster;
# 0 pauses cold first-syncs entirely.
# SYNC_MAX_COLD_REPOS_PER_TICK=5
